#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""glaeda-cmux-runner-hook: job hooks for the glaeda cmux Mac mini runner.

    glaeda-cmux-runner-hook job-started   [--allowed-repo O/R ...] [--allowed-owner O] [--disk PATH]
    glaeda-cmux-runner-hook job-completed [--disk PATH]

The runner calls these through ACTIONS_RUNNER_HOOK_JOB_STARTED and
ACTIONS_RUNNER_HOOK_JOB_COMPLETED (small .sh wrappers written by scripts/glaeda-cmux-runner).

job-started refuses (exits 1, so the runner fails the job before any step runs) when:
  * the event is not one of ADMITTED_EVENTS (so issue_comment, check_run, check_suite,
    pull_request_target and anything new are refused; --allow-event adds more)
  * the event is pull_request_target
  * the event carries a pull_request whose head repository is an unapproved fork, is missing, or differs
    from the base repository (covers pull_request, pull_request_review, and friends); teamleaderleo/cmux is the
    one approved fork
  * the event name starts with pull_request but the payload has no pull_request object
  * a workflow_run was triggered from a different head repository
  * the repository is not in the allowlist baked in at install time
  * GITHUB_EVENT_NAME or GITHUB_EVENT_PATH is missing or unreadable (fail closed)
  * with --trusted-ref REF --trusted-repo OWNER/NAME (a mini that holds a secret): the job is not a push
    or schedule of exactly that repository on REF (GITHUB_REF, the payload's ref and default branch)
Any crash also exits non-zero, which refuses the job. An admitted job is then held to the fleet host,
so a PR job never lands on a mini that is busy with other work. It is refused fast (the pool picker
re-runs it elsewhere) when:
  * the host is reserved: /Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/reservation.json (glaeda-reservation/v1, integer
    Unix seconds) with now < until, whoever owns it; an unreadable or invalid file also refuses, an expired
    one is ignored (parsed by glaeda_reservation.py, installed next to this hook)
  * free disk is below --min-free-gib even after one bounded glaeda-disk --pressure run evicted idle caches
    (evict; skipped with --no-disk). Above the floor but under the pre-job target (the floor plus
    PREJOB_MARGIN_GIB) it starts the queued pass before the job and waits up to PREJOB_WAIT_S for that target
    (prejob_evict), then admits it: the eviction happens before every job, not after it
  * the job needs the console session (a gui class job, or any job on a --gui-runner or --session-runner) and the
    console session is screen-locked or no user is logged in at the console (console_refusal, cmuxterm-hq#757)
    (a CONSOLE_SKIPPERS job is admitted as light instead: its take-gui step gives way and it skips)
  * another build holds the fleet host lock (/Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/host.lock, the flock that
    with-host-lock and the build worker take)
  * with --require-eligible (every fleet member): the local Glaeda node is not eligible, or its toolchain
    drifted from its class receipt, or gh is not on the job PATH. Eligibility is the node status that glaeda-mini-enroll reads, from the
    staged generation's cmux_fleet.py (state eligible, routingCandidateEligible true), and the enrollment
    must reference ~/.config/glaeda/cmux-fleet/class-acceptance/<class>.json. The toolchain check runs
    rustc, cargo, zig, xcodebuild and xcrun and compares them with that receipt's strings, exactly. First,
    rustup, rustc or cargo that cannot run at all (missing, or a link that dangles) refuses by path
    or is not on the job PATH, which is the runner's own (toolchain_missing); the gate then holds every such runner at once, and ci-step fits
    advertises nothing.
  * with --toolchain-xcode APP (the pool Xcode): the runner's environment sets TOOLCHAINS or an SDKROOT outside APP
    (leaked_toolchain_env; the xcrun shim's own SDKROOT does not count), or, with --require-eligible, xcrun in the
    job's clean env resolves an SDK outside APP, a swiftc of another Swift than APP's toolchain, or an SDK of a newer
    major than APP's, cannot `xcrun --find` a tool cmux uses (CMUX_TOOLS), or the PATH's python3 or the
    /usr/bin/python3 xcrun shim does not start (xcode_refusal, one probe batch: "toolchain: xcrun resolves ...
    outside Xcode_26.6"). Only a definite mismatch refuses; a probe that times out or prints nothing is no answer. An admitted job's later steps get
    DEVELOPER_DIR=APP/Contents/Developer through $GITHUB_ENV when the runner's env does not already set it.
Otherwise it takes that lock for the job: a small detached holder keeps it until job-completed releases
it or the job's Runner.Worker exits, so fleet builds wait for the PR job and a crash cannot strand it.
With --capacity-units N (several runners on one mini, docs/CMUX_MINI_RUNNER.md 2d) it takes a weighted
share instead: a shared lock on host.lock plus units and tokens under FLEET_DIR/capacity (take_capacity). When
they are taken a cmux job waits for them, with no time limit, and logs what it waits for and who holds it
(CAPACITY_WAIT); another repository's job waits --guest-wait seconds and then refuses with "refused: capacity: ...".
Only then does it trigger the same queued glaeda-disk --pressure pass job-completed does (below), never able to fail
the job and never holding it.

An admitted job also gets a detached host sampler (unless --no-telemetry): it records load, the cores used by
this job, by the other runner slots' jobs and by everything outside them, and the top outside processes, and
writes one "completed" JSON line per job to ~/Library/Logs/glaeda-cmux-jobs.jsonl (glaeda-cmux-job/v1,
"contended" or "clear"); job-started also writes a "started" line at admission and a "refused" line when it
refuses. After a warmup, a sustained low-CPU job may release surplus capacity units (and a compile-gui root)
through its holder; each release is logged as a "capacity_release" line. The existing load gate remains the
safety brake. glaeda-fleet-status reads the completed lines (source "jobs").

job-completed stops the sampler (it writes the record), starts the same disk pressure pass detached (bounded
by its own timeout, so the runner takes its next job at once) and always exits 0. One such pass runs at a time per
host: a trigger at or above glaeda-disk's target is skipped, and one that arrives while a pass runs is coalesced
into that pass's single follow-up (both logged in glaeda-disk's run log as "runner hook" skips). A
cmux compile admission may leave a bounded `glaeda-compile-telemetry.json` in `RUNNER_TEMP`; the completed
record embeds its cache and phase counters after validating the run identity.

    glaeda-cmux-runner-hook admit   --class C --job-key K --watch-pid PID --capacity-units N
    glaeda-cmux-runner-hook release --job-key K
    glaeda-cmux-runner-hook fits    --capacity-units N

are the build worker's ci-step admission (cmuxterm-hq#794): admit takes class C's units and tokens now for the
step K and leaves them with a holder that watches PID, or refuses without waiting (exit 1); release lets them go;
fits names the step classes this mini could admit right now. Each prints one JSON line (step_admit, step_fits).

    glaeda-cmux-runner-hook listen --runner-dir DIR [--no-waiters]

runs under the runner LaunchAgent's listen.sh: it runs DIR/run.sh, and while the fleet has the host (an exclusive
host lock, a reservation, or a fleet build waiting for the lock), every capacity unit on the mini is
taken, or the mini is saturated (sampled host CPU at least GATE_CPU_PAUSE or elevated memory pressure), it stops the idle
runner's listener so GitHub routes the job to another runner instead of here to be refused or slowed
down (Gate). A --require-eligible runner also stops listening while job-started would refuse every job for
eligibility (Gate.ineligible: the node status or the class receipt's toolchain, looked at in a thread at most
every GATE_ELIGIBLE_EVERY_S; a new refusal holds once a recheck agrees, and a held answer survives a hook reload),
so its label's jobs go to an eligible runner, or wait in GitHub's queue when none is, instead of failing in "Set up
job"; that look includes the pool Xcode's toolchain (xcode_refusal), so a host whose xcrun resolves past its Xcode
leaves admission. The gate starts run.sh with listener_env (no SDKROOT or TOOLCHAINS, DEVELOPER_DIR on the job-started
hook's --toolchain-xcode) and scrubs its own environment the same way; a listener it adopted on a reload whose env
was not that (adopted_env_stale) is stopped once idle and started clean.
A gui runner also stops listening while the console session is locked or nobody is logged in there
(console_refusal). A gui runner is never held for CPU saturation, only memory pressure (Gate.saturated), and while it
is idle its gate holds the gui runner's reserve (GUI_RESERVE: capacity/<gui token>.reserve): every other job on the
mini, and every other runner's gate, leaves one unit free for its next job, so the side and root lanes cannot fill
the mini and keep the gui label queued. A fast runner (--fast-runner) runs cmux-next's fast-tier on the fast tokens
(FAST_TOKENS) instead of the shared units, and its gate looks at those tokens, never at the units. Every runner also stops listening while free disk is under its job-started hook's --min-free-gib,
and starts the same eviction in the background, listening again once it made room (Gate.low_disk). Full means every
unit this runner's own --capacity-units scans, read from its job-started hook, is taken or kept for an idle gui
runner's reserve; for a root runner it also means the mini cannot admit a compile or an app-host shard (mini_full).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import datetime
import fcntl
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import math
import os
import platform
import plistlib
import re
import select
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, Callable, NamedTuple

DISK_TIMEOUT_S = 120  # a pass a hook or the gate waits on or reaps
# job-completed's detached pass, which nothing waits on: a whole pass took 192-685 s on cmux14 under the floor, so at
# DISK_TIMEOUT_S every one (a pass per job) was killed mid-apply while holding evict.lock, and the LaunchAgent's pass,
# which has no bound, skipped as busy (274 of 277 passes on 2026-10-26, none finished for over an hour)
BACKGROUND_DISK_TIMEOUT_S = 15 * 60
BACKGROUND_DISK_TIMEOUT_MAX_S = 6 * 3600  # an override above this is cut to it, so the bound's lifetime stays finite
BACKGROUND_DISK_TIMEOUT_ENV = "GLAEDA_RUNNER_BACKGROUND_DISK_TIMEOUT"  # GLAEDA_RUNNER_DISK_TIMEOUT bounds the others
# job-started waits at most this long for an eviction under the disk floor. At 60 s it only ever waited out part of
# the queued pass job-completed starts (hook passes took 125-534 s on 2026-10-26, median about 250 s), and its own
# pass then skipped as busy because the queue's next pass took evict.lock first: on cmuxs-mac-mini-6 at 05:18Z a
# nightly build-nightly-app was refused at 23.8 GiB ("waited 60 s for a running glaeda-disk pass; glaeda-disk exited 0,
# freed -1.0 GiB") while the pass in flight freed 16.2 GiB 100 s later. evict() now waits for running passes and
# retries its own while it skips as busy, within this budget.
EVICT_TIMEOUT_S = 300
EVICT_BUSY_PAUSE_S = 2.0  # between a pass of evict()'s own that skipped as busy and its next look
# The free disk a job is refused under, after eviction: the measured disk growth of one CMUX job, rounded up (p99
# 10.8 GiB, max 18.7 GiB). The runner floor (--min-free-gib, the manifest's disk.min_free_gib, 30 GiB since HQ #967, a
# judgement rather than a measurement) still decides when to evict, the pre-job target and when the gate stops
# listening; it no longer refuses a job that fits. A runner that takes cmux's tours keeps the tour floor
# (runner_floor_gib), which cmux measured for those jobs. glaeda-cmux-job/v1 completed lines record disk_free_start_gib,
# disk_free_end_gib (#1650) and disk_free_min_gib, so this can be resized from build-nightly-app's own peak.
DISK_JOB_PEAK_GIB = 20.0
DISK_JOB_PEAK_ENV = "GLAEDA_RUNNER_JOB_DISK_GIB"  # the runner's own environment may set another, 0 to 10000 GiB


def job_disk_gib() -> float:
    """DISK_JOB_PEAK_GIB, or DISK_JOB_PEAK_ENV when it is a finite number from 0 to 10000."""
    try:
        value = float(os.environ.get(DISK_JOB_PEAK_ENV) or DISK_JOB_PEAK_GIB)
    except ValueError:
        return DISK_JOB_PEAK_GIB
    return value if math.isfinite(value) and 0 <= value <= 10000 else DISK_JOB_PEAK_GIB


DISK_LOW = "disk-low"  # capacity/disk-low.lock and capacity/disk-low-<pid>: jobs admitted under the runner floor


def disk_low_turn(capacity_dir: Path, worker: int) -> str | None:
    """Admit at most one job at a time between the admission floor and the runner floor: the capacity ledger counts
    cores, not disk, so two jobs admitted together at 25 GiB could write past the disk between them (each may write
    up to DISK_JOB_PEAK_GIB). Under capacity/disk-low.lock (taken without blocking, retried for ADMISSION_WAIT_S), a
    marker of another job refuses this one unless that job is proven gone: its pid is dead, or alive with another
    start identity (process_identity). An unreadable marker or an identity that cannot be read refuses too. Otherwise
    this job's Worker leaves its own marker. Returns the refusal, or None (admitted, marker written)."""
    try:
        capacity_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        lock = os.open(capacity_dir / f"{DISK_LOW}.lock", os.O_RDONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o644)
    except OSError as error:
        return f"cannot open the disk ledger ({type(error).__name__})"
    try:
        deadline = time.monotonic() + ADMISSION_WAIT_S
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                break
            except OSError:
                if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                    return "another admission holds the disk ledger"
                time.sleep(0.1)
        for marker in capacity_dir.glob(f"{DISK_LOW}-*"):
            name = marker.name[len(DISK_LOW) + 1:]
            if not name.isdigit():
                continue  # not a marker (disk-low.lock, a .tmp being written)
            pid = int(name)
            if pid == worker:
                continue
            gone = not 1 < pid < 2 ** 31 or not pid_alive(pid)
            if not gone:
                try:
                    identity = (read_bounded(marker) or b"").decode("utf-8").strip()
                except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                    if not os.path.lexists(marker):
                        continue  # removed meanwhile
                    return f"cannot read the disk ledger's marker for pid {pid}"
                current = process_identity(pid)
                if not identity or current is None:
                    return f"another job may still run here under the disk floor (pid {pid}, identity unknown)"
                if current == identity:
                    return f"another job admitted under the disk floor still runs here (pid {pid})"
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                marker.unlink()  # its job is proven gone
        identity = process_identity(worker)
        if identity is None:
            return f"cannot pin this job's Runner.Worker (pid {worker}) for the disk ledger"
        mine = capacity_dir / f"{DISK_LOW}-{worker}"
        tmp = mine.with_name(f".{mine.name}.tmp")
        tmp.write_text(identity + "\n")
        tmp.replace(mine)
        return None
    except OSError as error:
        return f"cannot write the disk ledger ({type(error).__name__})"
    finally:
        os.close(lock)


def admit_floor_gib(min_free_gib: float, takes_tours: bool) -> float:
    """The free disk a job is refused under after eviction: the runner floor, capped at one job's measured disk
    growth (job_disk_gib), except on a runner that takes cmux's tours, whose floor is cmux's own measure."""
    return min_free_gib if takes_tours else min(min_free_gib, job_disk_gib())
# Before every job, not after it (Leo, 2026-10-26: "Just throw it away. Just delete more stuff, more often"): free disk
# is brought to the pre-job target, the floor plus PREJOB_MARGIN_GIB, before a job is admitted. Only checking the floor
# admitted several jobs at 31 GiB under a 30 GiB floor, and together they wrote the disk down to 0.1 GiB while every
# pass skipped as busy (cmuxs-mac-mini-6, 2026-10-25 18:46-19:02). Above the floor a job waits at most PREJOB_WAIT_S
# for the queued pass and is then admitted; under it, evict() and the floor refusal (the job runs elsewhere) apply.
PREJOB_MARGIN_GIB = 20.0  # glaeda-disk's target_margin_gib, the room it frees above the floor
PREJOB_MARGIN_ENV = "GLAEDA_RUNNER_PREJOB_MARGIN_GIB"
PREJOB_WAIT_S = 30.0
PREJOB_POLL_S = 1.0
# The runner's own environment (its LaunchAgent), never a job's env:, which does not reach hooks.
FLEET_DIR = os.environ.get("GLAEDA_FLEET_DIR") or "/Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet"
HOST_LOCK = f"{FLEET_DIR}/host.lock"
RESERVATION = f"{FLEET_DIR}/reservation.json"
HOLDER_FILE = "host-lock-holder"
HOLDER_POLL_S = 2.0
RELEASING = ".releasing"  # a released holder's pid file until it exits, so no admission mistakes it for a waiter
HOLDER_MAX_S = 5 * 24 * 3600  # GitHub's limit for a self-hosted job; the job's own exit releases it sooner
ORPHAN_HOOK_POLL_S = 2.0
# Weighted capacity (--capacity-units > 0, several runners per mini). Each job's class, from GITHUB_JOB
# (the job id in cmux's ci-macos.yml; matrix shards share it), costs units plus exclusive tokens.
# Compile admission's persistent DerivedData (/Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/ci, owned_build_state.py)
# takes one writer at a time, so a compile holds the persistent-dd token; GUI jobs share one console
# session (and tests-build-and-lag runs pkill -x "cmux DEV"), so they hold the gui token. An unknown
# job is treated as a compile: the safe side of both.
JOB_CLASSES = {"macos-compile-admission": "compile", "app-host-unit-tests": "gui-step", "tests-build-and-lag": "gui",
               "cli-product-tests": "product", "swift-package-tests": "light",
               # side lanes: no GUI, no persistent state. cli-pipe-regressions builds only cmux-cli, into
               # its own $RUNNER_TEMP DerivedData; remote-daemon-macos-tests is go test; claude-wrapper is node.
               "cli-pipe-regressions": "light", "remote-daemon-macos-tests": "light", "claude-wrapper": "light",
               # side-lane workflows (runs-on vars.CI_SIDE_LANE_RUNNER, glaeda-side-*): swift test or swiftc into
               # the workspace .build or a temp dir; no GUI, keychain, fixed port or canonical root (cmux audit
               # 2026-09-25). iroh-v2 `client`; cloud-command-deadlines `command-regressions`; terminal-hang-
               # diagnostics `portal-reconciliation` and `phase-attribution`; `lifecycle` is both
               # cloud-task-local-tests and cloud-machine-tests; relay-tls `diagnostic-presentation`;
               # auth-refresh-tests. Generic ids: a new job reusing one must be light too, or be renamed.
               "client": "light", "command-regressions": "light", "portal-reconciliation": "light",
               "phase-attribution": "light", "lifecycle": "light", "diagnostic-presentation": "light",
               "auth-refresh-tests": "light",
               # iOS (test-ios, ios-screenshots; runs-on glaeda-ios-sim): builds into their own DerivedData or
               # SwiftPM .build, never /private/tmp/cmux-ci, so no canonical root. Simulator jobs reuse, erase and
               # boot named devices in the user's one CoreSimulator service, so one per mini (the simulator token).
               # ios-streamed-validate's `validate` is not listed: fixed ports, GUI session changes and secrets in
               # $HOME keep it on Blacksmith (docs/CMUX_MINI_RUNNER.md 2g).
               "mobile-core-package": "isolated", "ios-simulator-build": "isolated",
               "ios-simulator": "simulator", "screenshots": "simulator",
               # ci-macos.yml release-build: an unsigned universal Release into its own workspace DerivedData,
               # never /private/tmp/cmux-ci*; no GUI, product or secrets (audit in cmux#14417). It runs
               # xcode-select -s host-wide, harmless while every job on the mini uses the pinned Xcode.
               "release-build": "isolated"}
# Job ids other workflows reuse, keyed by (workflow file, job id) and checked before JOB_CLASSES, since a bare
# id such as `build`, `test` or `lint` means different work in different workflows. cmux-tui's macOS jobs are
# cargo lint, test and its reusable build-artifacts `build` (GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF names the caller), into the
# workspace target dir: no GUI or canonical root. app-host-test-rerun's `rerun` claims the product's root itself
# (cmux#14417: glaeda-canonical-root take <root> --wait) before touching its DerivedData and runs its tests in
# the console session, so it is gui like app-host-unit-tests.
# test-e2e's `build` compiles into $CMUX_CI_CANONICAL_ROOT/derived-data-compile-admission (the root its token
# picks) and then runs the selected cmuxTests through run-in-console-session.sh, or cmuxUITests in the console
# user's GUI bootstrap, on the same runner (cmux "ci: run E2E tests on the runner that built them"), so it is
# compile-gui. It never writes compile admission's persistent DerivedData: on an owned Mac it only clones this
# root's kept state (owned_build_state.py check/prefer/adopt, cmux#14692), never keep/save/record, and only a
# job holding root-k writes root-k's store, so the root token covers it and it takes no persistent-dd token.
# Its `test` runs only when `build` did not test, and
# restores that product into the producer's root (restore-app-host-test-product.sh takes it) for the same
# console-session tests, so it is gui like `rerun`.
# seed-derived-data.yml's `seed` (trusted runners only) builds a DerivedData seed in the canonical root its
# matrix names (the root is part of the seed key), so no token may pick it: the job holds that root itself
# with glaeda-canonical-root take (cmux "seed far main pushes in their own lane") before it clears it. It
# never touches compile admission's kept DerivedData, so no persistent-dd either. Two seed jobs then fit
# on one trusted mini with two runners, ten units (cores) and two canonical roots.
# nightly.yml's `build-nightly-app` (trusted runners only, cmux "build the nightly app on the trusted owned
# minis first") compiles the universal Release app into its own workspace (build-universal, cleared at job
# start) and restores its own compile cache lineage: no canonical root, no kept DerivedData, no GUI. As an
# unknown id it would take the compile class and hold the root a seed on the same mini waits for.
# nightly.yml's `build-nightly-ghostty-cli-helper` (cmux: owned macOS 26 instead of macOS 15) runs Zig in the
# workspace's ghostty/ with its own Zig and helper caches under $HOME: no canonical root, Xcode DerivedData or GUI.
# It takes the light class (no token) at its measured cores (JOB_CORES), not a whole isolated slot.
# cmux-tui's `cdp-browser-smoke` drives a headless Playwright Chromium from cargo tests in the workspace: no
# console session or canonical root. Its `macos-relay` job runs the chatmux-relay Rust tests in the workspace
# and has the same no-root contract. reload-build's `build` is a Debug build of the dev app (or the iOS app)
# into the workspace's .ci-reload-derived-data, after git clean -ffdx: no GUI, keychain or canonical root.
# Both reach the minis as side lanes (vars.CI_SIDE_LANE_RUNNER, cmux docs/ci-runners.md "Side lanes").
# cmux-next.yml (cmux's feat-cmux-next branch only) is a side lane too: `swift-test` builds and runs the
# CmuxNext package's Swift Testing suites in the workspace .build (after checkout's git clean), and
# `release-compile` is an xcodebuild Release compile into $RUNNER_TEMP plus, on a mini, the CEF shim build
# into ~/Library/Caches/cmux/cef (content-addressed, rename-into-place). `cmux-scheme-compile` is a Debug
# xcodebuild of the cmux app scheme into a DerivedData kept per runner under
# ~/Library/Caches/cmux-next-ci/cmux-scheme/<runner> (cmux "cmux-next CI: compile the cmux app scheme"). No GUI
# session, keychain, fixed port or canonical root, and none touches compile admission's kept DerivedData.
# `fast-tier` (cmux hq#1915) and `swift-canary` build SwiftPM packages and cargo tests in the workspace only,
# so they are isolated too; as unknown jobs they waited for persistent-dd behind a compile.
# cmux-next-frame-pacing.yml (cmux#16511, nightly, side lane, owned minis only) calls reload-build.yml, so its
# `build` is reload-build's isolated Debug build under the caller's name. Its `bench` unpacks that app into
# $RUNNER_TEMP and flings the agent pane in the console session on a 120 Hz virtual display: it takes the gui
# token itself (glaeda-canonical-root take-gui --wait 900, the test-e2e `test` pattern) before the console
# steps, so it is gui-step. It restores no product into a canonical root.
# cmux-next.yml's `daemon-test` and `generated-files`, and the autofix workflows' `action-exports`
# (cmux-next-pr-autofix.yml, cmux-next-base-autofix.yml), run on the mini's gui runner for its logged-in session,
# not the console token: each builds the CmuxNext package with swift build --build-tests into the workspace .build
# (daemon-test and generated-files link it to owned_spm_scratch.py's per-workspace store) and runs swift test
# there. No canonical root, no kept DerivedData, no XCTest, so they are isolated like `swift-test`. As unknown jobs
# they were pinned compile on root 1 (daemon tests 17.8 h a day, generated files and exports 15.9, hq#2546), so two
# could never run on one mini however many gui runners it had.
# cmux-next-media.yml's `tour` (cmux pr-media, on a session runner) builds the merge base and head as tagged dev
# apps into per-tag DerivedData (~/Library/Developer/Xcode/DerivedData/cmux-ci-media-pr-<N>-{base,head}, one pair
# for merge tours) and captures each app's own window (its window snapshot, else `screencapture -l` by window id),
# driven through its socket and never activated: no canonical root, no kept DerivedData, no XCTest, no gui token,
# and nothing another tour's window can cover. The workflow holds the tour's tag for the job (cmux
# scripts/ci/media_tag_lock.py), so two tours never build into one tag directory ("ghostty.h has been modified
# since the module file was built", cmux#21686) and the hook needs no tour token: tours are isolated, as many on
# a mini as its units allow.
WORKFLOW_JOB_CLASSES = {("cmux-tui.yml", "lint"): "isolated", ("cmux-tui.yml", "test"): "isolated",
                        ("cmux-tui.yml", "build"): "isolated", ("cmux-tui.yml", "cdp-browser-smoke"): "isolated",
                        ("cmux-tui.yml", "macos-relay"): "isolated",
                        ("reload-build.yml", "build"): "isolated", ("app-host-test-rerun.yml", "rerun"): "gui",
                        ("test-e2e.yml", "build"): "compile-gui", ("test-e2e.yml", "test"): "gui-step",
                        ("seed-derived-data.yml", "seed"): "seed",
                        ("nightly.yml", "build-nightly-app"): "isolated",
                        ("nightly.yml", "build-nightly-ghostty-cli-helper"): "light",
                        ("cmux-next.yml", "swift-test"): "isolated", ("cmux-next.yml", "release-compile"): "isolated",
                        ("cmux-next.yml", "cmux-scheme-compile"): "isolated",
                        # Fleet gateway clients (runs-on vars.CI_COMPILE_CHECK_GATEWAY): `cmux-ci compile-check`
                        # submits to the Linux pool and waits for its verdict (up to about 4 min), no build on this
                        # mini. As unknown jobs they were compiles and held persistent-dd and a root while waiting,
                        # so a real compile on the same mini waited behind them (2026-10-24: "holding
                        # 5/14 units+persistent-dd+root-1 for release-compile-fast (compile"). The Mac fallback
                        # after a no-verdict is a separate job with its own class. Keyed by workflow: another
                        # workflow's job of the same id stays a compile.
                        ("cmux-next.yml", "release-compile-fast"): "light",
                        ("cmux-next.yml", "swift-canary-fleet"): "light",
                        ("cmux-next.yml", "app-compile-fleet"): "light",
                        ("cmux-next.yml", "fast-tier"): "isolated", ("cmux-next.yml", "swift-canary"): "isolated",
                        ("cmux-next-frame-pacing.yml", "build"): "isolated",
                        ("cmux-next-frame-pacing.yml", "bench"): "gui-step",
                        ("cmux-next.yml", "daemon-test"): "isolated", ("cmux-next.yml", "generated-files"): "isolated",
                        ("cmux-next-pr-autofix.yml", "action-exports"): "isolated",
                        ("cmux-next-base-autofix.yml", "action-exports"): "isolated",
                        ("cmux-next-media.yml", "tour"): "isolated"}
# root: the canonical root (/private/tmp/cmux-ci) that compiles build in and that app-host test consumers
# restore a product into with rm -rf of its src, so one root job per root per mini (cmux#14338 adds a second).
# product (cli-product-tests) runs its XCTest bundle through the same user testmanagerd as the gui jobs, so it
# holds the gui token too: one XCTest run at a time per mini, which lets a gui job restart that daemon
# (recycle_testmanagerd) without cutting another job's run.
# compile-gui takes no gui token at job start: the step that decides whether the build tests takes it with
# glaeda-canonical-root take-gui (cmux#14705), and leaves the tests to test-e2e's `test` job when take-gui gives
# way or times out. So its compile leaves the console session to other GUI jobs.
# gui-step (app-host-unit-tests) is a gui job that, on a mini with more than one root, takes the gui token itself,
# with take-gui in the step before its restore step, instead of at job start. Its 70 to 230 s product fetch then
# leaves the console session to other GUI jobs (a shard held the token for its whole job at about 0.13 cores,
# cmux CI 2026-09-26). It takes the token before the producer's root, the order gui jobs take them in, so it
# never holds a root while it waits for the token. A one-root mini gives it gui and the root at job start, as
# for gui, so a shard there still waits for a sibling's token (CAPACITY_WAIT) and take-gui is a no-op. A cmux
# revision without the take-gui step would run its tests without the token, so this class ships after that
# step (cmux#15012). test-e2e's `test` job is gui-step too (cmux#15037): it runs only when the build's take-gui
# could not get the token, so taking it again at job start would wait for it a second time.
# Units are cores: a mini's --capacity-units is its core count (cmuxterm-hq build-fleet/mini-fleet.json
# capacityUnits: 14 on an M4 Pro std mini) and a job's cost is the cores it uses, so a mini admits jobs until their
# estimated cores fill its cores. Costs are the p75 of cores.job (the job's own processes, mean cores over the job)
# in glaeda-cmux-jobs.jsonl, rounded up, from 14,421 jobs on the 11 std PR minis, 2026-10-12 20:24Z to 10-19 20:24Z,
# each job re-classed by these tables. By class (n, p50/p75/p90 cores): compile (3611) 3.96/4.53/5.20: 5;
# isolated (8597) 2.45/3.85/4.68: 4; compile-gui (415) 0.52/3.50/4.12: 4; light (304) 0.69/2.58/3.75, but
# every light job on a std mini has its own JOB_CORES cost, and the side-lane jobs left to the class measured on the
# light minis (glaeda-side-*, p75 1.33 there; std side runners take them too since side_classes()): 2;
# gui-step (852) 0.45/0.58/0.69, simulator (378) 0.18/0.28/0.42, product (195) 0.17/0.28/0.35 and gui (69)
# 0.23/0.32/0.60: 1. seed has no PR-mini telemetry (trusted minis only) and compiles a whole DerivedData: a
# compile's 5. The simulator and gui tokens, not units, keep those jobs one per mini. isolated is also the class
# guests default to and the one a co-resident dev build admits under (cmuxterm-hq
# build-fleet/cmd/worker/coresident.go, about 7 cores): that build checks the mini's measured headroom for its own
# footprint first, so the ledger's 4 is a floor for it, not its estimate. JOB_CORES gives a job its own cost where
# its p75 rounds to another number than its class's (20 runs or more in the window). The listener gate's CPU marks
# (GATE_CPU_PAUSE, GATE_CPU_RESUME) catch what a p75 estimate misses: a job above its p75, and processes outside
# any job (about 1 core on a std mini).
# fast: a FAST_JOBS job on the mini's fast runner (--fast-runner, the fast pool label; glaeda_fleet_labels
# fastRunners). Its two units are the fast tokens, kept apart from the shared unit-N files, so it never waits
# behind side-lane jobs for a unit and no other class, gate or ci-step ever counts them. The same job on any other
# runner keeps its WORKFLOW_JOB_CLASSES cost.
FAST_TOKENS = ("fast-0", "fast-1")
FAST_JOBS = {("cmux-next.yml", "fast-tier")}
CLASS_COST: dict[str, tuple[int, tuple[str, ...]]] = {
    "compile": (5, ("persistent-dd", "root")), "gui": (1, ("gui", "root")), "product": (1, ("gui", "root")),
    "compile-gui": (4, ("root",)), "gui-step": (1, ("gui", "root")),
    "isolated": (4, ()), "simulator": (1, ("simulator",)), "light": (2, ()), "seed": (5, ()),
    "fast": (0, FAST_TOKENS)}
# A job's own cost in cores, where it is far enough from its class's: keyed by (workflow file, job id) for the home
# repository, (repository, workflow file, job id) for a guest. Only units change: the job keeps its class's tokens
# (cli-regressions is a compile at 0.16 cores and still holds persistent-dd and a root). Same window as CLASS_COST;
# the comment is the job's n and p50/p75/p90 cores.
